with q as (
  select $l6_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The master equation of propulsion beyond chemistry is F = 2P/v_e. At a FIXED power P, what does it say about thrust and exhaust velocity?", "options": ["They rise together \u2014 more exhaust velocity means more thrust", "They are inversely proportional \u2014 every increase in exhaust velocity buys a proportional DECREASE in thrust; you cannot have both without more power", "They are independent of each other", "Thrust does not depend on the power source"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Thrust is momentum per second (F = m-dot\u00b7v_e) and power is energy per second (P = \u00bd\u00b7m-dot\u00b7v_e\u00b2). Solving the power relation for the mass flow and substituting gives F = 2P/v_e: at fixed power, thrust falls as v_e rises. Physically, energy scales as velocity squared but momentum only linearly, so pouring a fixed energy budget into faster exhaust lets you throw only a little mass \u2014 little momentum, little thrust. Want ten times the exhaust velocity? You get one-tenth the thrust. It is a consequence of definitions, as inescapable as the rocket equation."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Dawn accumulated 11.5 km/s of \u0394v \u2014 more than a launch to orbit \u2014 on 425 kg of xenon, yet its engine produced only ~90 millinewtons (the weight of two coins). What does this reveal about ion missions?", "options": ["Ion engines are simply inefficient and waste most of their power", "The resource economy inverts: propellant becomes abundant (mass ratios near 1) while TIME and POWER become the scarce resources \u2014 Dawn had to thrust continuously for years", "Ion engines can be used to launch from Earth", "The rocket equation does not apply to ion drives"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "An ion mission flips Unit I's economy. Propellant is almost a non-issue \u2014 Dawn's 425 kg of xenon did the work of ~31 tonnes of chemical propellant \u2014 but the feeble thrust (an acceleration under one hundred-thousandth of a g) means the engine must fire for years to accumulate the \u0394v, and thrust is proportional to power (F = 2P/v_e), which fades as 1/r\u00b2 from the Sun. So the questions become 'how many years of thrusting?' and 'how many kilowatts?' rather than 'how much propellant?' This is why ion propulsion rules deep space and satellite station-keeping \u2014 patience-rich jobs \u2014 and is absent from launch."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why did five chapters of chemical rocketry never mention the power\u2013thrust trade, and why must every ion mission still begin with a chemical rocket?", "options": ["Chemistry is exempt from the laws of physics", "Combustion delivers absurd power density (the Saturn V's ~43 GW of jet power), so at chemistry's modest v_e the thrust F = 2P/v_e is still colossal \u2014 the trade never bites; leaving chemistry collapses the power to kilowatts, so ion thrust is far too weak to lift off a planet", "Chemical rockets do not produce thrust", "Ion engines are heavier than chemical ones"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Chemistry's real gift was never its exhaust velocity \u2014 it was its power density. The propellant IS the energy source, and combustion releases energy so ferociously (gigawatts) that even at a capped v_e of ~4.5 km/s the thrust is meganewtons. The instant you bring a separate power source \u2014 a solar array is kilowatts, six-plus orders of magnitude weaker \u2014 the F = 2P/v_e trade bites savagely, and pushing v_e to 30 km/s drops the thrust to millinewtons. You cannot overcome your own weight with that, so a chemical rocket must always do the launch; the ion drive only takes over in space."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Nuclear thermal propulsion runs COOLER than a chemical engine (~2,900 K vs ~3,600 K) yet achieves nearly double the exhaust velocity. How, and why does it keep high thrust when ion drives cannot?", "options": ["It burns a special high-energy fuel", "It attacks the molecular-weight term: heating pure hydrogen (M = 2) with fission instead of burning a heavy oxidizer, and since v_e \u221d \u221a(T/M), slashing M from ~18 to 2 overwhelms the lower temperature; and a fission reactor's gigawatt power density keeps thrust high (F = 2P/v_e doesn't bite)", "It uses electric fields like an ion drive", "It runs at higher temperature than chemistry"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Exhaust velocity goes as \u221a(T/M). Chemistry is floored near M \u2248 10\u201318 because it must carry a heavy oxidizer into its own exhaust. Nuclear thermal abandons combustion and heats the lightest possible gas \u2014 pure hydrogen, M = 2 \u2014 with a fission reactor, and the molecular-weight advantage (a factor of ~9 inside the square root) crushes the modest temperature disadvantage. Crucially, a reactor delivers gigawatts, comparable to combustion, so unlike a feeble-powered ion drive it gets high v_e AND high thrust \u2014 the best of both worlds, which is exactly what a crewed Mars mission wants."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "NERVA \u2014 a nuclear thermal engine achieving about 850 s of specific impulse \u2014 was built, tested, and worked, then cancelled in 1973. Why, and what is meant by calling the engine \"a medical device\"?", "options": ["It exploded on the test stand; the phrase is ironic", "It was cancelled for lack of a mission, not any technical failure \u2014 the crewed Mars program it was built for was defunded; it is called a medical device because a nuclear rocket's fast transit cuts the crew's galactic-cosmic-ray dose, so its true product is reduced radiation and cancer risk", "It was banned by an international treaty", "It was too heavy to launch"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "NERVA performed \u2014 doubling chemical specific impulse, demonstrating restart, throttling, and hours of run time, essentially flight-ready. It was cancelled because by 1973 the crewed Mars program had lost political support and budget; the engine for a trip nobody was taking became a line item to cut. The general lesson: a technology's fate is set by whether the institution funding it still wants the thing it does, not by whether it works. It is being revived now because crewed Mars is being considered again \u2014 and the deep reason is radiation: cosmic-ray dose is dominated by transit time, shielding works poorly, so the best countermeasure is to go faster, which needs nuclear thermal's high thrust and high v_e. Its real output is fewer astronaut cancers."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter says breaking the exhaust-velocity wall reveals a \"third hard variable\" and forces the high-performance future to be nuclear. Explain both claims using the master equation F = 2P/v_e.", "rubric": "The rocket equation has two hard variables \u2014 exhaust velocity and the mass ratio \u2014 and for five chapters those were the whole story. Escaping chemistry reveals a THIRD: thrust. Chemistry handed thrust over for free through its absurd power density (combustion is a gigawatt-scale energy source), so the trade between thrust and exhaust velocity stayed hidden. The master equation F = 2P/v_e makes it explicit: at a fixed power, thrust and exhaust velocity are inversely proportional, so you cannot have both without enormous power. The moment you leave chemistry, the power source collapses by orders of magnitude (a solar array is kilowatts vs combustion's gigawatts), and the trade bites \u2014 ion drives, with feeble power, are forced to high v_e and negligible thrust. So breaking the v_e wall does not make spaceflight easy; it RELOCATES the difficulty from 'not enough exhaust velocity' to 'not enough power to have both exhaust velocity and thrust.' The only way to get high thrust AND high v_e at once is prodigious power, and the only compact prodigious power source is nuclear (nuclear binding energy is ~10^6\u201310^7 times denser than chemical bonds). That is why every serious high-thrust-high-v_e concept \u2014 nuclear thermal, nuclear electric, fusion, Orion \u2014 is nuclear. It is not a coincidence but a theorem, and F = 2P/v_e is its proof: the constraint is thermodynamic, not technological."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "There are exactly two ways to break the chemical exhaust-velocity wall, suited to opposite jobs, and the chapter offers a single skill for judging any propulsion proposal. Lay out the two routes, why each suits its job, and the skill \u2014 and connect it to the unit's through-line.", "rubric": "Two routes, because there are exactly two ways to break the wall. (1) Change HOW you accelerate the exhaust: electric fields instead of heat \u2014 ion propulsion, v_e \u2248 30 km/s (ten times the wall), by ionizing atoms and accelerating them electrically (voltage sets the speed, with no thermal or molecular-weight ceiling). It chooses high v_e and feeble thrust, so it is useless for leaving a gravity well but supreme for the patient, propellant-starved work of deep space \u2014 which is why it has taken over deep-space science, geostationary station-keeping, and megaconstellations. (2) Change the WORKING FLUID and heat source: heat pure hydrogen (M = 2) with a fission reactor instead of combustion \u2014 nuclear thermal, v_e \u2248 9 km/s (double the wall) at high thrust, because the reactor's gigawatt power keeps F = 2P/v_e from biting. It suits crewed missions that need both reasonable transit time and a sane mass ratio. The skill: judge every proposal with two tools \u2014 the rocket equation (does the \u0394v close if v_e is high enough?) and F = 2P/v_e (does the power source permit the claimed thrust AND v_e?). Ask of any engine: what is the power source, and does the trade allow what they claim? That sorts real from fantastical \u2014 why ion drives are feeble (weak power), why antimatter is forbidden by the stockroom not the physics, why Orion was killed by a treaty not a theorem, and why the high-performance future is unavoidably nuclear. The through-line \u2014 never carry anything through a \u0394v it does not need to experience \u2014 reaches its purest expression here: ion propulsion carries almost no propellant at all (mass ratio near 1), defeating the exponential not by dropping mass along the way, as staging does, but by needing so little mass to begin with. High exhaust velocity is the ultimate way to carry less."}]$l6_quiz$::jsonb as questions
